To add an item to the **FlightData** map context menu and detect the click location in a Mission Planner plugin, you use the `PluginHost` properties `FDMenuMap` and `FDMenuMapPosition` [1].

Here is how you can implement this:

### 1. Accessing the Menu
Inside your plugin's `Init()` or `Loaded()` method, access the `FlightData` map context menu via the host:
```csharp
// Get the FlightData map context menu
ContextMenuStrip fdMapMenu = Host.FDMenuMap;
```
The `FDMenuMap` property retrieves the `contextMenuStripMap` from the main `FlightData` control [1].

### 2. Adding a Menu Item
Create a standard Windows Forms `ToolStripMenuItem`, assign a click event handler, and add it to the menu:
```csharp
var newMenuItem = new ToolStripMenuItem("My Custom Map Action");

newMenuItem.Click += (sender, e) => {
    // Retrieve the location where the user right-clicked
    PointLatLng clickedLocation = Host.FDMenuMapPosition;

    // Example: Use the location
    Console.WriteLine($"Clicked at Lat: {clickedLocation.Lat}, Lng: {clickedLocation.Lng}");
};

fdMapMenu.Items.Add(newMenuItem);
```

### 3. Retrieving the Click Location
When the user right-clicks the map, `FlightData` captures the coordinates and stores them in its internal `MouseDownStart` field [1]. The plugin can access this exact coordinate via:
```csharp
PointLatLng location = Host.FDMenuMapPosition;
```
This property returns the `PointLatLng` corresponding to the mouse position when the context menu was invoked [1].

### Complete Example Snippet
```csharp
using System.Windows.Forms;
using MissionPlanner.Plugin;
using MissionPlanner.Geospatial;

public class MyPlugin : Plugin
{
    public override bool Init()
    {
        // Create the menu item
        var mapItem = new ToolStripMenuItem("Do Map Action");

        // Attach handler to read click position
        mapItem.Click += (s, e) => {
            PointLatLng pos = Host.FDMenuMapPosition;
            // Handle 'pos'...
        };

        // Add to FlightData map menu
        Host.FDMenuMap.Items.Add(mapItem);

        return true;
    }
}
```

